From a testing perspective, the framework goes in the right direction but feels a bit heavy and not very practical to run day-to-day. There are a lot of tests listed, but no clear thresholds or guidance on what actually matters, so it’s hard to know when something is a real issue versus noise. It also leans quite theoretical in places—for example, tests like WEAT or embedding association metrics are useful for analysing base models, but are often not that relevant when you’re using GenAI in a controlled application (e.g. summarisation, case narratives, or copilots), where the real risk sits in outputs and decisions rather than word embeddings. Similarly, some prompt-based bias tests can be over-engineered if the use case is narrow and well-defined. Another important point is proportionality: if GenAI is just one component in a pipeline and the upstream model (e.g. a risk score or TM model) has already been properly tested for bias and fairness, then the GenAI layer should be treated more as a presentation or transformation layer, with lighter-touch testing focused on output quality, consistency, and absence of harmful content. Overall, the strategy would benefit from being simpler, more outcome-driven, and more pragmatic—focusing on a small number of meaningful tests, clear thresholds, and adapting the level of effort to the actual risk and role of GenAI in the end-to-end process.
